  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 3,
  "question": {
    "text": "Two simple harmonic waves having equal amplitudes of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> and equal frequency of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Hz</mi></math> are moving along the same direction. The resultant amplitude is also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. The phase difference between the individual waves is _____ degree.",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "120",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>If two waves have the same amplitude, frequency, and direction, but are out of phase by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#981;</mi></math>&nbsp;degrees, then the amplitude of the resultant waves is given by&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>A</mi><mi>cos</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>&#981;</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfenced></math></p><p>Here,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p><p>So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>cos</mi><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>&#981;</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>Or phase difference between&nbsp;the individual waves is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>&#981;</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>&#981;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>120</mn><mo>&#176;</mo></math>.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
